# IAS37 - Provisions, contingent liabilities and contingent assets

IAS37.6 - An entity shall recognise a provision only when:
(a) an entity has a present obligation (legal or constructive) as a result of a past event;
(b) it is probable that an outflow of resources embodying economic benefits will be required to settle the obligation;
(c) a reliable estimate can be made of the amount of the obligation.
If these conditions are not met, no provision shall be recognised.

IAS37.13 - A present obligation exists only when an entity has incurred an obligation in the past by:
(a) placing itself under a legal or contractual obligation either by entering into an agreement with another party or parties or by the action of a third party, or
(b) operating in a particular way that has created a valid expectation in other parties that it will discharge the obligation.

IAS37.22-25 - Provisions should reflect:

a) The risk and uncertainty associated with the obligation;

b) The future events that may affect the amount required to settle the obligation (e.g. changes in technology, legislation);

c) The time value of money, where the effect is material.
When discounting is used, the discount rate(s) used should be pre-tax rate(s) that reflect the current market assessments of the time value of money and the risks specific to the liability. The discount rate(s) should not reflect risks for which future cash flow estimates have been adjusted.

IAS37.26-29 - When determining a provision, an entity should take into account:
a) the possible or probable outcomes of any event(s) that may confirm the existence of a present obligation and the amount and timing of any resulting outflows;
b) the range of possible outcomes and the most likely outcome(s) in each case.
Where a large population of items is involved, the obligation is determined by weighting all possible outcomes by their associated probabilities. The provision will therefore be different depending on whether the probability of outflow for each item is assessed as greater or less than 50%.

IAS37.36-43 - A legal or constructive obligation to make reimbursements (e.g. via an insurance policy) can exist even when the insurance policy only covers part of, or all of, the cost of the settlement of the obligation. An entity does not net amounts receivable from third parties against a provision.

IAS37.46 - The amount recognised as a provision shall be the best estimate of the expenditure required to settle the present obligation at the end of the reporting period.

The best estimate of the expenditure required to settle the present obligation is the amount that an entity would rationally pay to settle the obligation at the end of the reporting period or to transfer it to a third party at that time.

IAS37.47 - An entity shall not recognise a contingent liability. Contingent liabilities are either disclosed or ignored.

IAS37.52 - Contingent assets are possible assets that arise from past events and whose existence will be confirmed only by the occurrence or non-occurrence of one or more uncertain future events not wholly within the control of the entity.

IAS37.53 - Contingent assets are not recognised. They are either disclosed or ignored.

IAS37.57 - Where discounting is used, the increase in the provision due to the passage of time shall be recognised as a borrowing cost.

IAS37.59 - Where an entity expects some or all of a provision to be reimbursed, the expected reimbursement shall be treated as a separate asset. The amount recognised for the expected reimbursement shall not exceed the amount of the provision.

IAS37.70 - An entity shall disclose for each class of provisions (unless the effect would be impracticable) the following:
a) the carrying amount at the beginning and end of the period;
b) additional provisions made in the period, including increases to existing provisions;
c) amounts used (i.e. incurred and charged against the provision) during the period;
d) unused amounts reversed during the period;
e) the increase during the period in the discounted amount arising from the passage of time and the effect of any change in the discount rate.

IAS37.72 - An entity shall disclose the following for each class of provisions:
a) a brief description of the nature of the obligation and the nature of the uncertainty about the amount or timing of any outflows;
b) an indication of the expected uncertainties (where not disclosed under b);
c) the assumptions made concerning the future events;
d) the amount of any expected reimbursement, stating the amount of any asset that has been recognised for that expected reimbursement.

